      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14231667/assets/8af022dcc3065a036ba04fb79e75c43906ccef7f32e3416917778afec3d344a8.png)\n\nGiven that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=\\frac{7}{3},$ calculate the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=\\frac{7}{3}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AC} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{4x-1}{x+1} \\\\ 7(x+1) & =3(4x-1) \\\\ 7x+7 & =12x-3 \\\\ 7x-12x & =-3-7 \\\\ -5x & =-10 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =x+1=2+1=3, \\\\ BC & =4x-1=4(2)-1=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
